-- title:
--   Integral classes span after flat base change
-- statement:
--   Let $N > 0$, let $n \ge 0$, and let $R$ be a commutative ring that is flat over $\mathbf{Z}$.
--   Then every class $\Phi \in H^1_c(\Gamma_1(N), \operatorname{Sym}^n R^2)$ is an $R$-linear combination
--   $$\Phi \;=\; \sum_{i=1}^{m} c_i \cdot \varphi_i^{R}, \qquad c_i \in R,$$
--   of the coefficientwise extensions $\varphi_i^R$ of finitely many **integral** classes
--   $\varphi_i \in H^1_c(\Gamma_1(N), \operatorname{Sym}^n \mathbf{Z}^2)$, where $\varphi \mapsto \varphi^R$
--   applies the map $\mathbf{Z} \to R$ to the coefficients of the binary forms.
--
--   This is the **surjectivity** half of flat base change for compactly supported modular symbols. Together with
--   injectivity — which follows from flatness and the fact that unimodular paths generate, so that the integral
--   lattice embeds in a finite-rank free module — it yields the canonical isomorphism
--   $$R \otimes_{\mathbf{Z}} H^1_c(\Gamma_1(N), \operatorname{Sym}^n \mathbf{Z}^2) \;\xrightarrow{\ \sim\ }\;
--   H^1_c(\Gamma_1(N), \operatorname{Sym}^n R^2).$$
--
--   The mathematical content is Manin's **presentation**, not merely his generation theorem: one needs that
--   $\operatorname{Div}^0(\mathbf{P}^1(\mathbf{Q}))$ is finitely *presented* over $\mathbf{Z}[\Gamma_1(N)]$ —
--   generated by the unimodular paths, with all relations consequences of the two-term relation
--   $\{g\sigma\} + \{g\} = 0$ and the three-term relation $\{g\} + \{g\tau\} + \{g\tau^2\} = 0$, where
--   $\sigma = \begin{pmatrix}0&-1\\1&0\end{pmatrix}$ and $\tau = \begin{pmatrix}0&-1\\1&-1\end{pmatrix}$.
--   Given such a presentation, $H^1_c(\Gamma_1(N), \operatorname{Sym}^n -)$ is the kernel of a fixed matrix with
--   integer entries acting on a free coefficient module of finite rank, and flatness of $R$ makes the formation of
--   that kernel commute with $- \otimes_{\mathbf{Z}} R$, which gives the displayed spanning statement.
--
--   Flatness cannot be dropped: for a non-flat $R$ the natural map $R \otimes_{\mathbf{Z}} H^1_c(\mathbf{Z}) \to H^1_c(R)$
--   is in general neither injective nor surjective.
-- source:
--   Ju. I. Manin, Parabolic points and zeta functions of modular curves, Izv. Akad. Nauk SSSR Ser. Mat. 36 (1972), 19-66, §1.5-1.7 (presentation of Div^0 by Manin symbols with the two- and three-term relations); Ash-Stevens, Modular forms in characteristic l and special values of their L-functions (1986), §4, Definition 4.1 and Proposition 4.2, pp. 861-863, https://math.bu.edu/people/ghs/papers/Mod_fms_char_ell.pdf.

import Definitions.Def_MTT_Cohomology
import Mathlib.RingTheory.Flat.Basic
set_option autoImplicit false
noncomputable section
open scoped BigOperators TensorProduct
open MTT.Cohomology

theorem MTT.Cohomology.integral_classes_span
    {N n : ℕ} (hN : 0 < N) (R : Type*) [CommRing R] [Module.Flat ℤ R]
    (Φ : Hc N n R) :
    ∃ (m : ℕ) (c : Fin m → R) (φ : Fin m → Hc N n ℤ),
      ∀ D : Cusp × Cusp,
        Φ.val D = ∑ i, c i • MvPolynomial.map (Int.castRingHom R) ((φ i).val D) := by sorry
